ere in between and transitioning  from one mode to the next it's you know  whatever is going to get that code out  there into the hands of the people  that need to use it that's what we do  what makes ibm so motivated to help  sponsor the open source  scientific computing ecosystem and  specifically the pi data conference  series  there are several different reasons so  in a big partly because a lot of the  [Music]  users of ibm products are working with  the pi data stack  anyone who is using cloud pack for data  anyone who is using watson studio  anyone who's using our spectrum  computing high performance computing  stack  they are going to be using pi data  technology on um stuff  stuff managed by num focus under the  covers  and it's very important for us as a  company to maintain a good connection to  our users because at the end of the day  our products are about making those  users productive so that they can get  stuff done  for their employers who are the ones who  are buying this  these products from us and  having a good connection to pi data gets  us that connection to all those data  scientists and all those developers  and all those architects who are  building solutions on a daily basis  using ibm products  that's one reason that we like to be  connected strongly to the pi data  community  another reason is that we  incorporate a lot of pi data technology  in our products you know  things like jupiter and just python as a  whole  numpy is everywhere for example and  as a result of incorporating that we  also do a lot of contributions back  and if you're contributing code to  an open source project it's very  important to maintain a connection  to the community community of  contributors  and you at conferences like pi data  you'll see not just the users but also  the contributors  so we need to connect to maintain that  connection with the users  and to maintain that connection with the  contributors and of course we  also see a lot of thought leaders who  talk with the bi-data community and we  want to have a two-way communication  with them too  so it's very important to maintain a  connection to pi data  ibm sponsors uh dozens  of pi data events every year many many  meetups  the occasional conference we sometimes  donate to the projects themselves  we have a very active relationship going  back a long way  with pi data so fred i understand  ibm has directly supported linux  foundation ai specifically ai fairness  360 ai explainability360  and another initiative about model  robustness can you tell us a little bit  more about this  so ibm research has developed a lot of  technology around  in the area of trusted ai and we had  open sourced this technology as  a collection of toolkits as you were  saying ai fairness 360 ai explainability  360  adversarial robustness 360. and  these had been ibm ibm run open source  projects but  this earlier this year we donated them  to the linux foundation under the linux  foundation ai  initiative and we're really happy to  have those projects under open  governance  in general ibm is a big proponent of  open governance  we like to see all the members of the  industry working together  on open source we think that everyone  wins that way  and we're also really proud of this  technology it's an entire stack  for trusted ai for bringing trust and  transparency to ai development  for making models that aren't biased for  making models that are going to  resist attacks and generally  building up the level of trust that a  lot of our the people  the clients that we work with for  example in highly regulated industries  need to have around their models and  versions of this technology are in our  products but we think it's really  important  to have an open version and to maintain  that strong connection between them  so we're really proud to have donated  all that cutting-edge technology to the  linux foundation this year  so fred is there any final message you'd  like to leave 